\title[A Geometric Sieve of Eratosthenes]{A Geometric Sieve of Eratosthenes:
Least-prime-factor densities, perfect packings, and the integers as a point set}

\author*[1]{\fnm{Peter} \sur{Upham}}\email{founder@threebons.ai}
% \author[1]{\fnm{Peter} \sur{Upham}}\email{founder@threebons.ai}\orcid{https://orcid.org/0009-0004-9940-5859}

\affil[1]{\orgname{Independent Researcher}, \orgaddress{\country{USA}}}

\abstract{%
For each prime $p$, let $d_p$ be the natural density of integers whose least prime factor is $p$; these densities sum to $1$. We realize this partition geometrically by perfect packings with one piece of area $d_p$ per prime. In the axis-parallel similar-rectangle model we determine the exact feasible interval of container aspect ratios,
$1/\sqrt2+1/\sqrt6\le r\le\sqrt6$, using sixteen interval-certified finite schedules and an arrangement-independent infinite continuation criterion. Moreover, every shifted least-prime-factor state beginning at a prime $s\ge3$ admits a perfect square packing, and the state $2$ square packing is impossible; this supplies a recursive realization of the integer-state dissection. We also prove perfect self-similar packings of an aspect-$0.63$ ellipse and an isosceles right triangle, obtained from certified finite heads and explicit eroded-volume tail estimates. The resulting constructions represent the same arithmetic state structure in polygonal and smooth convex geometries. An exact staircase dissection also yields a limiting direction $\arctan(e^L)\approx56.29^\circ$ for an explicit convergent prime series $L$.%
}

\keywords{Perfect packing, Least prime factor, Prime density, Guillotine partition,
Self-similar dissection, Mertens' theorem}

