\section{Introduction}

Let $p_1=2<p_2=3<\cdots$ be the primes, and for a prime $p$ let
\begin{equation}\label{eq:dp}
d_p \;=\; \frac1p\prod_{q<p}\Bigl(1-\frac1q\Bigr)\;=\;\frac{\varphi(m)}{p\,m},\qquad m=\prod_{q<p}q,
\end{equation}
the natural density of $\{n\in\N:\lpf(n)=p\}$, where $\lpf$ denotes the least prime factor. The first values are
\[
d_2=\tfrac12,\quad d_3=\tfrac16,\quad d_5=\tfrac1{15},\quad d_7=\tfrac4{105},\quad
d_{11}=\tfrac8{385},\quad d_{13}=\tfrac{16}{1001},\;\ldots
\]
Writing $R_{<p}=\prod_{q<p}(1-1/q)$, the densities telescope---$d_p=R_{<p}-R_{<p'}$ for $p'$ the next prime---so that $\sum_{p\le P}d_p=1-R_{<P'}$; the tail $R_{<P'}\to0$ precisely when $\sum_p1/p$ diverges, hence
\begin{equation}\label{eq:total}
\sum_{p}d_p=1
\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad
\prod_p\Bigl(1-\frac1p\Bigr)=0
\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad
\sum_p\frac1p=\infty .
\end{equation}
The divergence of $\sum_p1/p$ is thus exactly what completes the partition.
The identity $\sum_p d_p=1$ is an immediate consequence of the telescoping in
\eqref{eq:total} together with Euler's theorem, both classical. Its explicit statement as a standalone partition of
unity appears to have been recorded at least once before, by Hicks and Ward \cite{HicksWard}, as a
curiosity. This paper takes it as the starting point of a geometric program: realize the partition
$\{d_p\}$ as a packing of a fixed planar shape and study
the resulting configuration.

There are several contributions. Section~\ref{sec:integers} first isolates the arithmetic recursion common to every geometric realization: a closed cell-area formula, a Markov state structure, and the shifted least-prime-factor families that appear inside every cell. Sections~\ref{sec:rect}--\ref{sec:packing} then prove the three packing theorems, all unconditionally. The similar-rectangle model admits a sharp classification for $1/\sqrt2+1/\sqrt6\le r\le\sqrt6$ (\S\ref{sec:rect}), via sixteen interval-certified guillotine heads and an arrangement-independent continuation estimate; the shifted families for all prime states $s\ge3$ admit perfect square packings, providing a recursive state realization. The aspect-$0.63$ ellipse is perfectly packed by similar ellipses of areas $d_p$ (\S\ref{sec:ellipse}), via an eroded-volume capacity bound that shares the rectangle's prime estimates and an interval-certified $1650$-piece head; Part B proves that the arithmetic state recursion propagates through smooth curved cells. The isosceles right triangle is perfectly packed by similar triangles of areas $d_p$ (\S\ref{sec:packing}) using the same eroded-volume capacity method, with mixed-area constant $\kappa_T=3/\sqrt2$, an interval-certified $10906$-piece head, and a family of $45$ per-state heads carrying the full recursion into triangular cells. Anisotropy emerges as the property a self-similar realization requires: the axis-parallel square and the disk fail. Section~\ref{sec:angle} studies the limiting direction of an exact staircase dissection, and Section~\ref{sec:realizations} collects the skeleton, graph-directed, and gauge consequences of the recursive realizations. Section~\ref{sec:realizations} also distinguishes the canonical recursive structure from pointwise gauge choices, while Section~\ref{sec:open} lists open problems.

\section{Asymptotics, literature, and terminology}\label{sec:models}

Write $R(x)=\prod_{p\le x}(1-1/p)$ for the density not yet claimed after sieving by primes up to $x$. Mertens'
third theorem \cite{Tenenbaum} gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mertens}
R(x)\;\sim\;\frac{e^{-\gamma}}{\ln x},\qquad e^{-\gamma}=0.5614594836\ldots
\end{equation}
Thus the geometric filling is logarithmically slow: the first $25$ pieces (primes through $97$) cover about
$88\%$ of the region, while covering $99\%$ requires all primes up to roughly $e^{56}\approx2\times10^{24}$.
Halving the uncovered area requires squaring the prime. In index form, $p_n\sim n\ln n$ gives piece areas and
linear scales
\begin{equation}\label{eq:index}
t_n=d_{p_n}\sim\frac{e^{-\gamma}}{n\ln^2 n},\qquad
s_n=\sqrt{t_n}\sim\frac{c}{\sqrt n\,\ln n},\qquad
\sum_n s_n=\infty,\quad \sum_n s_n^2=1 .
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Position in the perfect-packing literature.} Meir and Moser \cite{MeirMoser} asked whether the squares
of side $1/n$, $n\ge2$, perfectly pack a rectangle of area $\pi^2/6-1$; the problem remains open. For side lengths
$n^{-t}$, perfect packings of a square are known for $\tfrac12<t\le\tfrac23$ (Januszewski--Zielonka \cite{JZ2021},
after Chalcraft \cite{Chalcraft} and W\"astlund \cite{Wastlund}), and Tao \cite{Tao} reaches all $\tfrac12<t<1$ at
the cost of discarding finitely many initial squares; triangle analogues at exponent $\tfrac12+\epsilon$ appear in
\cite{JZtriangle}. Closest to the present setting, Sono \cite{Sono} generalizes Tao's theorem to side lengths
$f(n)^{-t}$ for suitable $f$---including $f(n)=p_n$, the $n$th prime---for every $\tfrac12<t<1$, with effective
bounds on the number of discarded initial pieces. The LPF side lengths in \eqref{eq:index} have the distinct
logarithmically corrected form $c/(\sqrt n\ln n)$; their late pieces are small relative to the remaining
area, and the principal geometric restriction comes from the largest prescribed pieces. This paper determines
that restriction sharply for the axis-parallel similar-rectangle model, and supplies complete constructions for
two further convex containers. In every model the identity $\sum d_p=1$ makes placement of the
entire sequence equivalent to a perfect packing, with residual area after the prime-$p$ head equal to $R(p)$.

\begin{definition}[Admissible placement; perfect packing]\label{def:perfect}
Fix a container $C$ of area $1$. A \emph{placement} assigns to each prime $p$ a closed set $P_p\subset C$ that is a
similar copy of $C$ of area $d_p$, the $P_p$ having pairwise-disjoint interiors. It is \emph{admissible} in a given
model if each $P_p$ arises by that model's allowed motions of $C$: for the similar-rectangle model
(\S\ref{sec:rect}), axis-parallel placements in either of the two axis-parallel orientations; for the
similar-ellipse model (\S\ref{sec:ellipse}), translations in the two axis-parallel orientations
($0^{\circ}$ or $90^{\circ}$); for the triangle model (\S\ref{sec:packing}), the eight axis-aligned
$45^{\circ}$ orientations. The placement is a \emph{perfect
packing} if $\bigcup_p P_p$ covers $C$ up to measure zero. Since $\sum_p d_p=1$, a placement of \emph{all} the
pieces has total area $1$, so disjoint interiors already force coverage up to measure zero: an admissible
placement of every piece is automatically perfect.
\end{definition}

