\section{The least-prime-factor recursion and state structure}\label{sec:integers}

Before choosing a geometric container, it is useful to isolate the arithmetic recursion that the packing must realize. At this stage we use $[n]$ for an \emph{abstract arithmetic cell}: its mass is the natural density of the integer set it represents, and its children are determined entirely arithmetically. Section~\ref{sec:rect} provides the first geometric realization of this state tree.

The prime cell $[p]$ represents $\{pm:\ m=1\text{ or }\lpf(m)\ge p\}$, so it carries, in miniature, a complete copy
of the sieve \emph{restricted to primes $\ge p$}; for $p=2$ this is the entire original pattern. The
restricted sieve is again complete at every level:
\[
\sum_{q\ge p}\frac1q=\infty\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\prod_{r\ge p}\Bigl(1-\frac1r\Bigr)=0 .
\]
Iterating, each integer $n\ge2$ with sorted factorization
$q_1\le q_2\le\cdots\le q_k$ names a nested chain of cells, and:

\begin{proposition}[Cell area]\label{prop:cell}
The abstract cell $[n]$ (representing $\{nm:\ m=1\text{ or }\lpf(m)\ge P(n)\}$, where $P(n)$ is the largest prime factor) has prescribed mass
\[
\operatorname{area}[n]\;=\;\frac1n\prod_{r<P(n)}\Bigl(1-\frac1r\Bigr).
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The cell represents $S_n=\{nm:m=1\}\cup\{nm:\lpf(m)\ge P(n)\}$, and we prescribe $\operatorname{area}[n]$ to be the natural
density of $S_n$; the packing theorems below realize these masses geometrically. The singleton contributes density $0$. The map $m\mapsto nm$
is a bijection onto the multiples of $n$ and scales density by $1/n$, so
$\operatorname{dens}(S_n)=\tfrac1n\operatorname{dens}\{m:\lpf(m)\ge P(n)\}$. Integers with no prime factor below
$P(n)$ have density $\prod_{r<P(n)}(1-1/r)$ (a finite product, by inclusion--exclusion over the finitely many
primes $r<P(n)$, as distinct-prime divisibilities are independent). Combining gives the formula.
\end{proof}

\noindent The descendant structure of an abstract cell depends only on the largest prime in its chain---the sieve forgets
everything but its last letter---which yields the state reduction used throughout the geometric constructions.

\begin{proposition}[Markov self-similarity]\label{prop:markov}
If $P(m)=P(n)$ then $[m]$ and $[n]$ carry identical descendant trees with identical relative masses. In any geometric realization whose layout
depends only on the state $P(\cdot)$, the corresponding cells are therefore similar with ratio $\sqrt{n/m}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The children of $[k]$ are the cells $[kq]$ for primes $q\ge P(k)$, and recursively their descendants; this data
depends on $k$ only through $P(k)$, so $P(m)=P(n)=p$ forces identical (abstract) sub-dissection trees with
identical area ratios at every node. A construction that places each child by a rule depending only on the state
$P$ realizes the two trees by similar geometric figures; the ratio is fixed by areas, $\operatorname{area}[m]/
\operatorname{area}[n]=(1/m)/(1/n)=n/m$ (the products $\prod_{r<p}$ cancel), giving linear ratio $\sqrt{n/m}$.
For $m=15,n=5$ ($p=5$) the ratio is $\sqrt{5/15}=1/\sqrt3$.
\end{proof}


For later use, fix a prime state $s$. Relative to a state-$s$ cell, the child indexed by a prime $q\ge s$ has area share
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sigma}
\sigma_s(q)=\frac1q\prod_{s\le t<q}\Bigl(1-\frac1t\Bigr)=\frac{d_q}{R_{<s}}\qquad(q\ge s\ \text{prime}).
\end{equation}
These shifted shares again form a partition of unity, $\sum_{q\ge s}\sigma_s(q)=1$. Thus every abstract cell presents the same least-prime-factor packing problem with a shifted starting state; state $2$ is the original family $\sigma_2(q)=d_q$. The arithmetic input is now complete: what remains is to show that these prescribed masses and state transitions can actually be realized inside geometric containers. We begin with a model in which the free region can be represented exactly at every finite step.

\section{Sharp aspect-ratio classification in the similar-rectangle model}\label{sec:rect}

Let the container have area one and aspect ratio $r\ge1$, with side lengths $\sqrt r$ and $1/\sqrt r$.  For each prime $p$, a piece is a rectangle similar to the container, of area $d_p$, placed axis-parallel and optionally rotated by $90^\circ$.  Its aligned side lengths are $\sqrt{rd_p}$ and $\sqrt{d_p/r}$.  The finite heads below use guillotine placements.  The infinite continuation uses an arrangement-independent configuration-space estimate and need not preserve a guillotine partition of the free region.

\begin{theorem}[Sharp aspect-ratio classification]\label{thm:rect}
An area-one rectangle of aspect ratio $r\ge1$ admits a perfect axis-parallel packing by similar rectangles of areas $d_p$, one for each prime, if and only if
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rect-range}
\frac1{\sqrt2}+\frac1{\sqrt6}\le r\le\sqrt6.
\end{equation}
Throughout this interval the shifted prime-state families $\{\sigma_s(q):q\ge s\}$, $s\ge3$, likewise admit perfect packings in similar parent cells, so the packing can be propagated through the integer-state recursion.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Necessity.}  Put $a=1/\sqrt2$ and $b=1/\sqrt6$.  Two axis-parallel rectangles with disjoint interiors must have disjoint projections onto at least one coordinate axis.  Testing the four orientation pairs for pieces $2$ and $3$, with the individual containment constraints, gives the necessary interval $a+b\le r\le1/b=\sqrt6$.  Conversely, the relevant two-piece heads fit at both endpoints: at the lower endpoint the two rotated pieces sit in adjacent columns, and at the upper endpoint piece $2$ is aligned while piece $3$ is rotated and spans the container height.  Intermediate parameters admit one of the orientation patterns $RR$, $RA$, or $AR$, where $R$ denotes rotation and $A$ alignment.

\emph{Uniform infinite continuation.}  Thereafter require every piece $q\ge5$ to be aligned.  Write
\[
T_{<q}=\sum_{p<q}\sqrt{d_p},\qquad
N_q=\#\{p<q:p\text{ prime}\},\qquad
C_{\max}=\sqrt6+1/\sqrt6.
\]
For an aligned candidate in the container, the area of admissible lower-left positions is at least the free area $R_{<q}$ minus the configuration-space exclusion regions of the earlier pieces.  An earlier aligned rectangle contributes cross coefficient $2$; an orthogonal rectangle contributes $r+r^{-1}\le C_{\max}$.  Since at most pieces $2$ and $3$ are orthogonal, a sufficient condition, independent of their positions, is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rect-tail-criterion}
\frac{2+2T_{<q}+(C_{\max}-2)(a+b)}{\sqrt{qR_{<q}}}
+\frac{N_q-1}{q}<1.
\end{equation}
The companion interval calculation checks this at every prime $10477<q\le10^7$;
the largest certified upper endpoint is $0.999832068$ at $q=10487$.
For $q\ge X_1=10^7$, Appendix~\ref{app:tail} establishes the three decreasing bounds
\[
\frac{3T_{\le q}+\sqrt2}{\sqrt{qR_{<q}}}<0.614,
\qquad \frac1{\sqrt{qR_{<q}}}
\le\sqrt{\frac{\ln q}{\beta q}},
\qquad \frac{N_q}{q}\le\frac{1}{\ln q}\left(1+\frac{1.2762}{\ln q}\right),
\quad \beta=e^{-\gamma}\left(1-\frac1{\ln^2 10^5}\right).
\]
In particular the left side of \eqref{eq:rect-tail-criterion} is at most
\[
\frac23(0.614)+
\left(2+(C_{\max}-2)(a+b)-\frac{2\sqrt2}{3}\right)
\sqrt{\frac{\ln X_1}{\beta X_1}}
+\frac{1+1.2762/\ln X_1}{\ln X_1}<0.481<1.
\]
Thus any disjoint head through $10477$, with subsequent pieces aligned, extends to all primes by induction.  Since $\sum_p d_p=1$, the resulting packing is perfect.

\emph{Finite interval cover.}  Affinely normalize the container to the unit square.  An aligned piece becomes a square of side $\sqrt{d_p}$, while a rotated piece has dimensions $(\sqrt{d_p}/r,r\sqrt{d_p})$.  For a fixed combinatorial guillotine schedule, free-rectangle widths are of the form $A+B/r$ and heights of the form $C+Dr$, with schedule-dependent coefficients.  The corresponding fit constraints are linear inequalities in $r$ after multiplication by the positive factor $r$ where appropriate.  Hence interval validity follows from checking the endpoints.

The companion certificate contains sixteen schedules of the first $1282$ prime pieces, through $10477$.  Their initial orientation choices belong to $RR$, $RA$, and $AR$; all pieces from $5$ onward are aligned.  Each interval's placement inequalities are checked at both endpoints in outward-rounded interval arithmetic.  The intervals overlap and cover the interior of \eqref{eq:rect-range}.  At the two algebraic extremes the necessary first-two-piece contacts are equalities, while the remaining $2563$ checked constraints retain certified positive lower margins of $3.71\cdot10^{-7}$ and $3.02\cdot10^{-7}$, respectively.  This closes the full interval, including its endpoints.

\emph{Shifted states.} Within a state-$s$ parent, $s\ge3$, align every child with its parent. Affine normalization then gives a square-packing problem with areas $\sigma_s(q)$. Eight interval-certified finite heads cover states $s=3,5,7,11,13,17,19,23$. The square capacity criterion holds from its first piece at $s=29$ and, by monotone state comparison, for every larger prime state. Its finite state windows extend to $50021$; beyond that point the square-state capacity is bounded above by the already certified rectangle criterion \eqref{eq:rect-tail-criterion}, which covers all primes through $10^7$ and beyond. State-$2$ cells (including $[2],[4],[8],\ldots$) are packed using the main aspect-$r$ rectangle construction, while state-$s$ cells with $s\ge3$ use their aligned-square construction. Inserting these packings recursively realizes the complete arithmetic state structure.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Certificate and logical scope]\label{rem:reproduce}
The proof uses finite, reproducible interval certificates for the sixteen rectangle schedules and eight square-state heads, together with explicit analytic bounds for their infinite continuations. The certified interval endpoints, schedule identifiers and tail maxima are recorded in the companion materials~\cite{Code}, whose verifier reconstructs the placement inequalities in outward-rounded interval arithmetic.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Why the continuation is robust]\label{rem:rect-status}
The free area before prime $q$ is exactly $R_{<q}$, while the new piece occupies only $d_q=R_{<q}/q$.  The configuration-space estimate \eqref{eq:rect-tail-criterion} uses only the piece areas, their prescribed orientations, and the container aspect ratio; it makes no assumption on the shape, number, or maturity of the remaining free components.  In particular, the finite heads need not leave a similar reserve or any special free-rectangle invariant.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Aspect ratio and recursive compatibility]\label{rem:rwindow}
The sharp interval \eqref{eq:rect-range} concerns the axis-parallel similar-rectangle model with rotations by $90^\circ$.  Its endpoints are forced by the initial two pieces; the finite interval cover and uniform continuation prove sufficiency for every intermediate ratio.  The shifted-state square theorem permits recursion inside similar cells throughout this range, although it does not require the initial head itself to possess a particular guillotine reserve.
\end{remark}

Figure~\ref{fig:rect} illustrates the established $r=\sqrt2$ packing.  The classification above is a statement about all ratios in \eqref{eq:rect-range}, not a claim that the displayed $\sqrt2$ schedule is valid unchanged at every ratio.

