\section{Perfect packing in the similar-ellipse model}\label{sec:ellipse}

The rectangle of \S\ref{sec:rect} gives an exact polygonal realization; we now pass to a smooth one.
Packed as \emph{similar ellipses} into an ellipse, the same areas $d_p$ again admit an unconditional perfect
packing. Part A proves the prime-level packing, and Part B then returns to the arithmetic recursion of
Section~\ref{sec:integers} and propagates the construction through every state, yielding a recursive realization
in smooth, curved cells. This is the model nearest the round shapes for which the question is classically posed. Throughout, the container is an ellipse of area $1$ and aspect ratio $r\in(0,1)$ (semi-axes in ratio
$1:r$), the piece for prime $p$ is the \emph{similar} ellipse of area $d_p$ in one of two axis-parallel
orientations ($0^{\circ}$ or $90^{\circ}$). The exact normalized mixed-area constants are $1$ for aligned ellipses
and $\kappa_*(r)=\frac{2}{\pi r}E(1-r^4)$ for orthogonal ones, where $E$ denotes the complete elliptic integral
of the second kind; write $\bar\kappa=(1+\kappa_*)/2$. We fix the exact rational aspect $r=0.6296271949192772$ for the certified head.
For each fixed $r>0$ the analytic continuation applies after a sufficiently large finite head.
Since $\sum_pd_p=1$, any placement of every piece is a perfect packing, and the whole problem is again to
place every piece.

Two structural differences from \S\ref{sec:rect} organize the section. First, ellipses do not tile: at every finite
stage the placed pieces leave a positive-measure residue of curvilinear gaps, so there is no exact reserve-and-fringe
ledger. We replace that exact ledger by an \emph{eroded-volume capacity}, a boundary-layer bound of
Steiner--Minkowski type that does not require the free region to be a union of prescribed shapes. Second, once this
geometric loss is bounded, the resulting placeability criterion reduces to the same arithmetic quantities already
introduced for the rectangle, namely $T_{<q}=\sum_{p<q}\sqrt{d_p}$ and $R_{<q}$, now weighted by $\bar\kappa$.
Thus the new work in this section is geometric; the infinite-tail estimates remain those of Appendix~\ref{app:tail}.

\subsection{Part A: the prime packing}

The isotropic limit $r=1$ is exactly where the model fails, which is the sharp form of why an aspect ratio is needed
at all.

\begin{proposition}[Isotropy obstruction]\label{prop:iso}
For $r=1$ (disks) no perfect packing exists: pieces $2$ and $3$ already cannot be placed. They are disks of radii
$a_2=(2\pi)^{-1/2}=0.39894$ and $a_3=(6\pi)^{-1/2}=0.23033$, so disjointness needs their centres at distance
$\ge a_2+a_3=0.62927$; but in the disk container each centre is constrained to the concentric disk of radius
$\pi^{-1/2}-a_i$, so the two centres are separated by at most $2\pi^{-1/2}-a_2-a_3=0.4991$. Hence $r=1$ is infeasible, and by continuity so is a
neighbourhood of it; anisotropy ($r\ne1$) is necessary. Together with the first-two-piece square obstruction
in Theorem~\ref{thm:rect}, this shows that the two isotropic containers, angular and round, are excluded.
\end{proposition}

The elongation of an ellipse is what removes this: the area-$\tfrac12$ piece lies along the container's long axis
and its neighbours turn $90^{\circ}$ to seat beside it, as in Figure~\ref{fig:ellipse}. We now make the placement
of every piece unconditional. The boundary tax is governed by the Minkowski mixed area \cite{Schneider}, for which
similar ellipses give a clean constant.

