\begin{theorem}[Perfect packing of the triangle]\label{thm:tripack}
The isosceles right triangle of area $1$ is perfectly packed by pairwise-disjoint similar copies of itself of areas
$d_p$, one for each prime.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Place the primes $\le115363$ by the certified head (Theorem~\ref{thm:trihead}); at the first tail prime $q_1$ the
free region has area $R_{<q_1}$. From $q_1$ onward run the disciplined schedule. Proposition~\ref{prop:tricross}
gives the averaged criterion \eqref{eq:tridisc}; by its construction, at least one of the two candidate orientations
then satisfies the underlying eroded-volume criterion \eqref{eq:tristar}, so a legal placement of the $q$-piece
exists and is taken. No piece is ever refused, and $\sum_pd_p=1$ forces coverage up to measure zero. The head is a
terminating exact/interval computation and the tail an explicit analytic bound (Appendix~\ref{app:tail}), so the
theorem is unconditional and computer-assisted in exactly the sense of Theorems~\ref{thm:rect} and~\ref{thm:ellipse}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[An exact algebraic base]\label{rem:tribase}
The first $15$ primes $2,\dots,47$ admit a stricter, fully symbolic certificate: a greedy placement of $15$
triangles of areas exactly $d_2,\dots,d_{47}$ with every coordinate in $\Q(\sqrt2,\dots,\sqrt{47})$, all $135$
containment sign-tests and $105$ separating-edge certificates checked by exact symbolic computation \cite{Code}, the
free region having area exactly $R_{<50}$. This anchors the interval head against exact algebraic arithmetic on its
first pieces.
\end{remark}

The prime-level packing is now established by the same head--tail architecture as in the ellipse, but with the
orientation averaging above replacing central symmetry. We therefore turn, as before, to the shifted state families.

\subsection{Part B: the integers}

As for the ellipse, similarity carries the recursive dissection of \S\ref{sec:integers} into the triangle: the cell
of $n$ is a similar triangle of area $\tfrac1n\prod_{r<P(n)}(1-1/r)$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:cell}), and the children
of a state-$s$ cell carry the shifted least-prime-factor family $\sigma_s(q)=d_q/R_{<s}$ of \eqref{eq:sigma}. What
must be checked is that each cell is perfectly packed by its children.

\begin{proposition}[State comparison and finite heads]\label{prop:tristates}
Run the disciplined capacity method in a state-$s$ cell: its criterion is \eqref{eq:tridisc} with $d,R,N$
restricted to primes $\ge s$ and the free ($p\le1000$) and alternating masses subtracted separately, each with its
own weight ($2$ and $\tfrac32$). As in Proposition~\ref{prop:states} every subtracted state term---the container
coefficient $3\sqrt{R_{<s}}$, the two weighted partial sums, and the count---is nonnegative and increasing in $s$,
so $\mathrm{Crit}_s\le\mathrm{Crit}_2$ termwise: state $2$ is the hardest, and every state's criterion holds past a
crossover $q_1^{(s)}\le115363$; each of the $45$ state criteria is in addition evaluated directly in \cite{Code},
not inferred from monotonicity alone. Exactly the $45$ prime states $s\le197$ have a nonempty head; their
sizes run from $10906$ (state $2$, Theorem~\ref{thm:trihead}) down to $398$ (state $197$) and total $118593$
pieces, while every state $s\ge199$ is covered by the tail from its first piece
($\max_q\mathrm{Crit}_{199}(q)=0.9984<1$). All $45$ heads are certified to the standard of
Theorem~\ref{thm:trihead}, least certified gap $4.2\times10^{-18}$ over the family; the crossovers, head sizes, and
per-state certificates with their manifest are available in the companion code \cite{Code}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[The integers in triangular cells]\label{thm:triintegers}
Given the $45$ certified state heads of Proposition~\ref{prop:tristates}, the isosceles right triangle admits the
full recursive dissection of \S\ref{sec:integers}: every cell $[n]$ is perfectly packed by its children, and along
each infinite chain the nested similar triangles shrink to a point, realizing the full integer recursion in triangular cells with no residue.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix a cell of state $s$. If $s\ge199$, Proposition~\ref{prop:tristates} gives its criterion for every $q\ge s$, so
the capacity method (as in Theorem~\ref{thm:tripack}) places all its children; if $s\le197$, place the certified
state-$s$ head first and run the capacity method past that state's crossover. Either way the children are disjoint
and fill the cell up to measure zero. The states are countable and each is handled once, so the whole tree is
packed. As in Theorem~\ref{thm:ellintegers}, the shrinking down every chain is uniform: each descent step
multiplies the cell area by $\sigma_s(q)\le1/q\le\tfrac12$, so a depth-$m$ cell has area at most $2^{-m}$ and
diameter $O(2^{-m/2})$, and every infinite chain converges to a point.
\end{proof}

