The triangle completes the three proved realizations and joins the ellipse as a \emph{non-tiling} solution. Because
similar triangles corner-anchored at $45^{\circ}$ do not tile the free region, the recursive construction requires a
family of per-state certified heads---$45$ here, ten required for the ellipse (Proposition~\ref{prop:states})---whereas the
rectangle's exact guillotine tiling serves every state with a single construction (\S\ref{sec:rect}). More
importantly, the same eroded-volume method has now closed both a smooth centrally symmetric body and a polygonal
non-centrally-symmetric one. The common mechanism is therefore the capacity estimate itself, with the geometry
entering through explicit boundary and mixed-area constants. The
next sections study geometric properties of the resulting dissections.

\section{The staircase limit angle}\label{sec:angle}

The packing results above establish existence by model-specific constructions. The rectangle also admits a particularly transparent exact staircase dissection whose asymptotic direction can be computed independently of the packing machinery. In that staircase dissection, index the primes by $j$ and let $A=\{p_1,p_3,p_5,\dots\}=\{2,5,11,17,\dots\}$ receive the
vertical cuts and $B=\{p_2,p_4,\dots\}=\{3,7,13,\dots\}$ the horizontal ones. After $n$ steps the remainder
rectangle has dimensions
\[
w_n=\prod_{\substack{j\le n\\ j\ \mathrm{odd}}}\Bigl(1-\frac1{p_j}\Bigr),\qquad
h_n=\prod_{\substack{j\le n\\ j\ \mathrm{even}}}\Bigl(1-\frac1{p_j}\Bigr),
\]
and the staircase vertices $v_n=(1-w_n,\,1-h_n)$ converge to the corner $(1,1)$ at distance
$\asymp1/\sqrt{\ln p_n}$ by \eqref{eq:mertens} (Figure~\ref{fig:angle}).

\begin{theorem}[Limit angle]\label{thm:angle}
The direction of approach converges: with $a_j=\ln\bigl(1-1/p_j\bigr)^{-1}$,
\[
\frac{h_n}{w_n}\;\longrightarrow\;\kappa=e^{L},\qquad
L=\sum_{j\ge1}(-1)^{j+1}a_j ,
\]
so $v_n$ approaches $(1,1)$ along a ray at angle $\theta^*=\arctan\kappa$ above the horizontal. Moreover
$|L-L_n|\le a_{n+1}\le 2/p_{n+1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $S_A=\sum_{p\in A}1/p$, $S_B=\sum_{p\in B}1/p$. Then $S_A+S_B=\sum_p 1/p=\infty$, while
$S_A-S_B=\sum_j(-1)^{j+1}/p_j$ converges by the alternating-series (Leibniz) test, since $1/p_j$ decreases
strictly to $0$ (no prime-gap bound is needed). From a divergent sum and a convergent difference, both
$S_A=\tfrac12((S_A{+}S_B)+(S_A{-}S_B))$ and $S_B$ diverge; therefore $w_n,h_n\to0$ and the limit point is the
corner. Next $\ln(h_n/w_n)=\sum_{j\le n}(-1)^{j+1}a_j$ is the partial sum of an alternating series whose terms
$a_j=\ln(1-1/p_j)^{-1}$ decrease strictly to $0$, hence converges by the same test, with
$|L-L_n|\le a_{n+1}\le 2/p_{n+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{figure}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\textwidth]{staircase_angle.png}
\caption{Left: the staircase vertices and the limiting ray of Theorem~\ref{thm:angle}. Right: convergence of the
aspect ratio $h_n/w_n$ to $\kappa=e^{L}$.}
\label{fig:angle}
\end{figure}

The alternating-series estimate also gives a direct numerical enclosure. With all primes to $10^{8}$
($n=\pi(10^8)=5\,761\,455$ terms), consecutive partial sums give
$L_{n-1}=0.4047892129\ldots<L<L_n=0.4047892229\ldots$, hence
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Lenclosure}
L\;\in\;[0.40478921,\;0.40478923].
\end{equation}
Thus the limiting aspect ratio and angle are determined, for example, as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:constants}
\kappa=e^L=1.4989865\ldots,\qquad \theta^*=56.29205\ldots^{\circ},
\end{equation}
with the displayed precision read against \eqref{eq:Lenclosure}. The theorem therefore separates two scales of
convergence: the staircase vertex approaches the corner at the Mertens rate $1/\sqrt{\ln p_n}$, whereas its
direction is controlled by the alternating-series remainder $O(1/p_n)$.

