The recursive state structure may also be viewed as a countable-state graph-directed iterated function system in the sense of
Mauldin--Urba\'nski \cite{MU}: states are primes, edges $p\to q$ exist for $q\ge p$, and the contraction ratio is
$\bigl((1/q)\prod_{p\le r<q}(1-1/r)\bigr)^{1/2}$. This formalism is not needed for the packing proofs, but it gives a convenient language for the equivariant point-set gauges introduced below.

\subsection{The skeleton: infinite ink, dimension one, dense closure}

Here the \emph{skeleton} of the dissection means the union of all its cut-boundaries---not the medial axis of
shape analysis.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:ink}
In the triangular realization, the total edge length of the dissection is infinite already at a single level of the recursion: the level-one perimeters are $(2+2\sqrt2)\sqrt{d_p}$ and $\sum_p\sqrt{d_p}\asymp\sum_p(p\ln p)^{-1/2}=\infty$, with partial sums
$\asymp\sqrt{P}/(\ln P)^{3/2}$. The ink required to reduce the uncovered area to $\varepsilon$ is
\[
\mathcal{L}(\varepsilon)\;\asymp\;\varepsilon^{3/2}\exp\Bigl(\frac{e^{-\gamma}}{2\varepsilon}\Bigr),
\]
and by tail-completeness the same divergence holds inside every cell: every neighbourhood is infinitely inked.
Nevertheless the skeleton is a countable union of segments, hence of Hausdorff dimension $1$ and measure zero;
after full recursion cell diameters vanish along every infinite chain, so the triangular skeleton is dense in the triangle.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
A level-one piece of area $d_p$ is similar to the unit-area container (legs $\sqrt2$, hypotenuse $2$, perimeter
$2+2\sqrt2$), whence perimeter $(2+2\sqrt2)\sqrt{d_p}$. By Mertens, $\sqrt{d_p}=\sqrt{R_{<p}/p}\asymp(p\ln
p)^{-1/2}$, and partial summation against $\pi(t)\sim t/\ln t$ (as in Appendix~\ref{app:tail}, Step~3) gives
$\sum_{p\le P}\sqrt{d_p}\asymp\sqrt P\,(\ln P)^{-3/2}\to\infty$. For the ink function, the uncovered area after
the primes $\le P$ is $R(P)=e^{-\gamma}\bigl(1+O(1/\ln P)\bigr)/\ln P$ by Mertens' third theorem, so
$R(P)=\varepsilon$ forces $\ln P=e^{-\gamma}\varepsilon^{-1}\bigl(1+O(\varepsilon)\bigr)$; substituting into the
partial sums,
\[
\mathcal L(\varepsilon)\;\asymp\;\sqrt P\,(\ln P)^{-3/2}
=\exp\Bigl(\frac{e^{-\gamma}}{2\varepsilon}+O(1)\Bigr)\Bigl(\frac{e^{-\gamma}}{\varepsilon}\Bigr)^{-3/2}
\;\asymp\;\varepsilon^{3/2}\exp\Bigl(\frac{e^{-\gamma}}{2\varepsilon}\Bigr),
\]
the $O(\varepsilon)$ in the exponent contributing only the bounded factor $e^{O(1)}$. Inside a cell of state $s$
the children carry the shifted areas $\sigma_s(q)=\tfrac1q\prod_{s\le t<q}(1-1/t)=d_q/R_{<s}$, and
$\sum_{q\ge s}\sqrt{\sigma_s(q)}\asymp R_{<s}^{-1/2}\sum_{q\ge s}(q\ln q)^{-1/2}=\infty$ by the same estimate.
Thus every cell contains infinite total edge length; since cell diameters vanish along every infinite chain
(Theorems~\ref{thm:ellintegers},~\ref{thm:triintegers}), the same is true in every neighbourhood. Finally the skeleton is a countable union of straight segments, hence has Hausdorff dimension $1$ and Lebesgue measure zero, and it is dense because the diameters of nested cells tend to zero.
\end{proof}

The divergence is specific to the LPF area sequence rather than to perfect infinite dissections in general. It also illustrates why the geometric information in the construction is carried by measure rather than cardinality: every state has the same countably branching descendant structure, while the cell masses distinguish the states quantitatively.

\subsection{Gauge freedom: where do the integers live?}\label{sec:gauge}

The canonical content of the construction is a measure isomorphism between $(T,\text{Lebesgue})$ and the space of
infinite nondecreasing prime chains with its density measure. Measure isomorphisms are defined only up to null
sets, and individual integers are null: almost every \emph{point} of $T$ corresponds to an \emph{infinite} chain,
while finite words (the integers) correspond to cells---regions, never points. Any pointwise location of $n$ is
therefore a convention. The centroid of $[n]$ is a poor convention: generically it lies interior to some
descendant cell and is thus already owned by an infinite chain extending $n$'s word. The only unclaimed real
estate is the skeleton, the measure-zero set where cell addresses collide: integers naturally live on the seams.

A pointwise convention can be made compatible with the recursion. If each cell is packed so that its residual tail accumulates at a distinguished vertex, define $x_n$ to be that vertex of $[n]$. Then
\[
x_{nq}\;\longrightarrow\;x_n\qquad(q\to\infty\ \text{through primes }q\ge P(n)).
\]
Injectivity of $n\mapsto x_n$ is an additional design constraint and is not automatic. A stronger choice is an \emph{equivariant gauge}: if $\varphi^{(n)}_q$ is the similarity carrying the container onto $[nq]$, require
$x_{nq}=\varphi^{(n)}_q(x_n)$. Then multiplication is represented by the same contracting similarities that generate the recursive cells, placing the point set $\{x_n\}$ within the graph-directed framework above.

The same gauge choices can be imposed in each of the three established recursive realizations. For the rectangle, there is also a useful two-sheet extension: two independently packed $\sqrt2$ rectangles may be glued along their long edges, reversing the initial piece-$2$ split. One may then represent a reduced fraction $m/n$ by the chord joining $x_m$ on one sheet to $x_n$ on the other; reflection across the seam realizes reciprocation. This construction is explored further in forthcoming work.

This distinction also separates two kinds of geometric statement. Properties of the cells themselves---their masses, nesting, state structure, and the dimension and measure statements proved above---are gauge-invariant. By contrast, properties of the selected points $\{x_n\}$, such as injectivity, angular distribution, adjacency, or the location of accumulation points, depend on the chosen gauge and should be treated as constrained design properties rather than consequences of the packing theorems.

\section{Open problems}\label{sec:open}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Beurling systems and anisotropic continuation.} For an increasing generalized-prime sequence $p_1<p_2<\cdots$ with $\sum_n1/p_n=\infty$, the weights $d_n=p_n^{-1}\prod_{j<n}(1-p_j^{-1})$ sum to one. Determine explicit conditions on such sequences guaranteeing a finite admissible head and an arrangement-independent continuation, and identify convex-geometric analogues under quantitative mixed-area or anisotropy assumptions. The classical primes provide one instance; the Meir--Moser/Basel-type endpoint is a distinct and potentially more rigid regime. Normalization alone does not imply geometric packability.
\item \textbf{Head-free proofs (all three models).} Each packing theorem still rests on a finite certified head:
the capacity and guillotine criteria fail on an initial range of primes, which is settled by computation rather
than analytically. Remove the head in any model---prove the placeability criterion from the first piece under some
schedule, or show a stationary shape law for the rescaled free region that forces it---turning the computer-assisted
theorems into head-free ones. For the triangle a distinct, purely geometric route is also open, though not needed
for the theorem: prove the fatness invariant $\mathrm{inradius}(F_q)\ge c\sqrt{R_{<q}}$ for a fixed $c>0$ (observed
$\approx0.40$), which gives placeability directly---the inscribed disk of a free region of inradius
$\ge a_q=\sqrt{R_{<q}/q}$ holds a similar copy of $T$ of that altitude---without the mixed-area accounting.
\item \textbf{Ellipse anisotropy and convex extensions.} The sharp rectangular interval is settled by Theorem~\ref{thm:rect}. For similar ellipses, determine the feasible aspect-ratio range, replacing the fixed certified example by interval-certified heads and a uniform mixed-area continuation estimate. More generally, seek sufficient convexity and anisotropy conditions for self-similar perfect packings of prescribed area sequences. The disk remains excluded by Proposition~\ref{prop:iso}.
\item \textbf{The ellipse residual.} The similar-ellipse packing (Theorem~\ref{thm:ellipse}) leaves a residual of
a different character from the rectangle's polygonal packing skeleton. The residual contains $\partial\Omega$,
so its Hausdorff dimension is at least $1$; no upper bound below $2$ follows from perfect packing alone, and the
tail theorem permits arbitrary legal choices at every step, so the dimension is a property of the packing, not of
$r$ alone. Determine the attainable spectrum
$D_{\min}(r)=\inf_{\mathcal P}\dim_H E(\mathcal P)\le D_{\max}(r)=\sup_{\mathcal P}\dim_H E(\mathcal P)$ over
admissible packings $\mathcal P$.
\item \textbf{Perfect chain packings.} Does a perfect packing exist in which the piece of each prime touches the
piece of the preceding prime? Any neighbourhood of a corner tip is coverable only by arbitrarily small, hence
arbitrarily late, pieces, so a perfect chain must revisit every corner on an infinite schedule; no obstruction is known.
\item \textbf{The constant $L$} of Theorem~\ref{thm:angle}: closed form or irrationality; more generally the
arithmetic of index-parity prime sums. Also: fluctuations of $\ln(h_n/w_n)-L$, i.e.\ the interleaved-class
imbalance $\sum_{A,\,q\le x}1/q-\sum_{B,\,q\le x}1/q$.
\item \textbf{Linear independence of $\{\sqrt{d_q}\}$ over $\Q$} (the pairwise ratios are square roots of
explicit rationals); consequences: no accidental collinearities among vertices of distinct scales in algebraic
designs.
\item \textbf{The point set $\{x_n\}$} under the equivariant gauge. Determine the closure $\overline{\{x_n\}}$
and its Hausdorff dimension via the pressure of the associated conformal graph-directed Markov system (states the
primes, edge $p\to q$ for $q\ge p$ with contraction $(1/q)\prod_{p\le r<q}(1-1/r)^{1/2}$, pressure vanishing at
$s=2$); in particular relate its finiteness parameter $\theta$---the abscissa of convergence of the pressure---to
the Mertens-rate scaling $s_n\asymp(\sqrt n\,\ln n)^{-1}$ of the pieces and to the critical exponent $t=\tfrac12$.
Also: the skeleton's box dimension, and angular equidistribution for the spiral design via $\sqrt p\bmod1$.
\item \textbf{The chain word.} A chain packing induces one letter per prime (which $45^{\circ}$ family, which
side, at the contact edge): the statistics of this four-letter word as an arithmetic object.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Methods note}
Packings, decompositions and audits were computed in Python by floating-point geometric construction
(\texttt{shapely}/GEOS), followed by exact symbolic or interval verification where stated;
prime sums used sieves to $10^{8}$ with Euler acceleration. The triangle's exact algebraic base
(Remark~\ref{rem:tribase}) was verified in \texttt{sympy} over $\Q(\sqrt2,\dots,\sqrt{47})$: exact areas,
$135$ containment sign-tests, and $105$ pairwise separating-edge certificates, all passing. The triangle
prime head ($10906$ pieces) and the family of $45$ per-state heads were built by floating-point greedy placement
and then certified independently, in the rational-edge frame, by exact-rational and $100$-bit interval arithmetic:
containment by per-vertex edge margins, disjointness by separating-axis witnesses, fail-closed, with least
certified gap $4.2\times10^{-18}$ over the family and every intended boundary contact an exact zero; the crossovers,
head sizes, per-state certificates, and their manifest are reproduced by the companion code \cite{Code}. ``Verified''
elsewhere means: union area equals summed area, maximal pairwise intersection $\le3\times10^{-18}$, containment
checked, and area ledgers conserved to $\le5\times10^{-14}$.

